imes 5^2\n\\]\n\\[\n150 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor common to both numbers:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(100, 150) = 2^1 \\times 5^2 = 50\n\\]\n\n","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,4340,1657,6785,1378,48342,25780,525,9363,315,2176,220,16,15,15,323,220,16,20,15,30,151645,198,151644,77091,198,1249,8253,1246,1657,6785,1378,48342,25780,525,9363,315,2176,220,16,15,15,323,220,16,20,15,11,582,1191,553,9271,279,12196,4185,49109,320,38,6484,8,315,220,16,15,15,323,220,16,20,15,382,5338,11,582,1477,279,10250,8168,8040,315,220,16,15,15,323,220,16,20,15,510,59,9640,16,15,15,284,220,17,61,17,1124,15136,220,20,61,17,198,59,921,59,9640,16,20,15,284,220,17,1124,15136,220,18,1124,15136,220,20,61,17,198,59,2533,785,479,6484,374,279,1985,315,279,15457,2355,315,1817,10250,8168,4185,311,2176,5109,510,59,9640,59,1318,90,38,6484,25547,16,15,15,11,220,16,20,15,8,284,220,17,61,16,1124,15136,220,20,61,17,284,220,20,15,198,59,2533],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nHow many positive two-digit integers are factors of both 100 and 150?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"dda1370929276e10a8bfdeacf75b0f26fda75151786abac77c005ee5fdd9f5f3"},"seconds":11.474204540252686,"seed":912305161607944673,"stop":"eos","text":"This means that the common factors of 100 and 150 are the factors of 50. Now, we list the factors of 50:\n\\[\n50 = 1 \\times 50, \\quad 2 \\times 25, \\quad 5 \\times 10\n\\]\nSo, the factors of 50 are: 1, 2, 5, 10, 25, and 50.\n\nNext, we identify which of these factors are two-digit numbers. The two-digit factors of 50 are 10, 25, and 50.\n\nCounting these factors, we see there are 3 two-digit factors.\n\nTherefore, the number of positive two-digit integers that are factors of both 100 and 150 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{3}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To determine how many positive two-digit integers are factors of both 100 and 150, we start by finding the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 100 and 150.\n\nFirst, we find the prime factorizations of 100 and 150:\n\\[\n100 = 2^2 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\\[\n150 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor common to both numbers:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(100, 150) = 2^1 \\times 5^2 = 50\n\\]\n\nThis means that the common factors of 100 and 150 are the factors of 50. Now, we list the factors of 50:\n\\[\n50 = 1 \\times 50, \\quad 2 \\times 25, \\quad 5 \\times 10\n\\]\nSo, the factors of 50 are: 1, 2, 5, 10, 25, and 50.\n\nNext, we identify which of these factors are two-digit numbers. The two-digit factors of 50 are 10, 25, and 50.\n\nCounting these factors, we see there are 3 two-digit factors.\n\nTherefore, the number of positive two-digit integers that are factors of both 100 and 150 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]","id":"prealgebra:567"}
